// Canonical sources: a failure that stops the program in the middle of an
// expression. Bump's choose can only fail and nothing catches it. When Bump
// fails, the return's x is already evaluated, but the statement never
// completes: the program answers the value of its last completed statement,
// b's 6, the value it stood on. The thesis defines fail as a fail state that
// backtracks to another path and leaves open what a program answers when no
// path is left; the standing value is the reading that depends on no machine.
// Answer: 6.
int Fails() { choose { fail; } , { fail; } return 5; }
int Bump(int n) { int k = n + 1; int f = Fails(); return k + f; }
int x = 4;
int b = x + 2;
return x + Bump(b);
